==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun|matters of record}}

# A fact or statement that is recorded in [[governmental]] records or in publications.
#* '''1906''', [[w:Andy Adams|Andy Adams]], "The Story of a Poker Steer,"
#*: When Dick Larkin asked to see his credentials, the inspector not only produced them, but gave the owner's name and the county in which the brand was a '''matter of record'''.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Inland Architect and News Record| volume=45| page=65| year=1905| passage=It is a matter '''of record''' in history that asphalt was used in the construction of a highway in Peru, being built by prehistoric races.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Engineering News-record| volume=85| page=805| year=1920| page=805| title=Memoirs of Capt. Andrew Talcott| passage=This has now been privately printed and deposited in several of the leading libraries as a matter '''of record'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Liberation Historiography: African American Writers and the Challenge of History, 1794-1861| page=1| author=John Ernest| year=2004| passage=Indeed, African American history, like all history, remains as much a matter of argument as a matter '''of record'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Science Translational Medicine| title=Business and Markets: Where’s All That Money Going? (Pharma Edition)| author=Derek Lowe| date=February 27, 2018| passage=My feelings about share buybacks in an R&D driven industry like this one have been a matter '''of record''' for some time, for all the good that does.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[fact]] that is contained within [[court]] [[file]]s or has otherwise been entered into [[evidence]] before the court.